Comparing 2 companies for each Multinational, SME and startup

Multinationals:
-----------------

NVIDIA
   - GPUs, AI chips, accelerated computing
   - Founded in 1993, HQ in California. 42,000+ employees worldwide
   - Doesn't have an Irish office, but has remote jobs for Ireland
   - They have an intern programme min 12 weeks paid
   - 93% of employees recommend working for NVIDIA
   - Good company to have on a CV
   - But it's a competitive environment and hard to get in

Samsung
   - Phones, TVs, chips, displays
   - Founded in 1969, HQ in Seoul. 267,000+ employees
   - Has an office in Dublin
   - They have a 12-month placement programme for college students. It's structured with workshops and mentoring
   - About 65% of employees recommend working for Samsung
   - Has a good structured programme, it's a big brand to have on a CV
   - But it's competitive and hard to get in


SMEs:
-----------------

Phorest
   - Salon management software, bookings, payments, marketing tools for hair and beauty salons
   - Founded in 2003 in Dublin. It has around 470 employees
   - HQ is in Dublin and they do remote and office work
   - They don't have a proper programme, but they've had interns before
   - About 75% of employees recommend working for Phorest
   - It has great culture, is Dublin-based, and good opportunities to learn
   - But it has no guaranteed intern spots and would have to reach out

XOcean
   - Marine robotics, they use unmanned boats to map the seabed and collect ocean data
   - Founded in 2017, HQ in Rathcor, Co. Louth. It has around 240 employees
   - No formal placement program, but they've been hiring loads with 300 new jobs announced in 2023
   - About 50% of employees recommend working for xocean and there are concerns about management and work life balance


Startups:
-----------------

Wayflyer
   - Fintech, they give fast funding to eCommerce businesses
   - Founded in 2019 in Dublin. It has around 500 employees
   - HQ is on Hatch Street, Dublin and they do remote and office work
   - Has a placement programme as of March 2026
   - About 75% of employees recommend working at Wayflyer
   - It's Dublin based and has good opportunities to learn from their placement programme
   - But since it's a startup it may be chaotic and reviews say there are concerns about management and too high of pressure

Strikepay
   - Digital tipping, QR codes and NFC tags that let customers tip staff via Apple Pay/Google Pay without cash or an app
   - Founded in 2020 in Dublin and is backed by Enterprise Ireland
   - It has a very small team of around 5-15 employees
   - Based in Dublin and has won a bunch of awards: Spider Awards, KPMG FS Awards, National Fintech Awards
   - But because it's so small there is no placement programme
